ting so did was  it exclusively men who were singing  these motets we know that women saying  in the 14th century  they probably saying motets it's again  it's it's hard to know but there's  there's enough evidence of women's  singers and some of them probably quite  talented and certainly courtly songs and  what heads are they're kind of funny  they're not courtly they're not really  about love as much they sometimes it can  be and they're also not exactly  religious though they can be they occupy  this strange category where they can be  about just really many different things  and that also makes it hard to place  them in a in a cultural context because  they're so different from each other  okay yeah I was actually was going to  ask were you able to in reading the  lyrics correlate any of those lyrics to  what was going on politically or  culturally or socially during that time  period  absolutely so some some Oh tests are in  praise of specific rulers whether they  be Pope's or kings and so then that  that's easy is okay well then maybe this  was for the occasion of this this Pope's  ordination or for this kings for a  particular celebration or something  though even then it's often hard to  pinpoint exactly which one you know  seven hundred years later but sometimes  they are in honor of saints and then the  Saints will have been dead for hundreds  of years or more and then you have to  ask yourself to whom is the saint  important now and why and it ultimately  you know tends to be political in that  way the and then you know the more some  motets are just really especially V  trees maquettes tend to just be social  critique you know a critique against the  groveling courtier so the person at  court who just has no independence they  just tow the line and wouldn't you  rather be your own person wouldn't you  rather think your own thoughts even if  it  and having less money and less prestige  and that kind of a piece because of its  universality is absolutely i