ury we often talk  about text painting which is when you  get a word like up or skies and you go  up the musical line goes up or or you  thing about the abyss and you go down  and that's a very local relationship  between word and tone and that doesn't  happen very much in medieval music and  so if you then look at medieval music  and ask whether it does the kinds of  things that later music does to relate  word in tome you see that it doesn't it  doesn't do that because there are later  things and so my question was well what  does it do and so I posit a broader  relation between word and tone that's  not on the level of the individual note  in the word but actually on the level of  the piece of music and it's broader  message and then I think about the ways  in which medieval intellectual  traditions like analogy support that  kind of relation so ultimately what  would you like the reader to take away  from your book I would like them to to  know that the people who wrote these  motets did so very carefully I did so  very playfully but also very seriously  that they were thinking about the  biggest issues of their day and and also  the biggest musical issues of their day  the new possibilities made available by  developments in notation new formal  possibilities and they were constructing  works of art that that put these things  into this created encounters I guess  between between important things in life  and important things in music and so  it's worth our time looking at these  with the kinds of detail and the kinds  of care that that really carefully  constructed works of art command thank  you so much for being here with us say  this has been fascinating thank you for  having me for more information about  professors I aru's niya and her work  please visit our website at Macmillan  report yale.edu be sure to join us again  for another  SOTA the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  an